<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="sec-gaussian-elimination">
  <title>Gaussian elimination</title>

  <p>
    Substitution works for small examples. Gaussian elimination is the systematic version: replace a system by an equivalent, simpler system.
  </p>

  <activity xml:id="act-back-substitution">
    <title>Reading a solution from an augmented matrix (U2-LO1, U2-LO2)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The following augmented matrix (see <xref ref="def-1-1-coefficient-matrices"/>) represents a system of linear equations in three unknowns <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, and <m>z</m>. Write the corresponding equations, then solve the system.
      </p>
      <p>
        <me>
          \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
          1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -5\\
          0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 2\\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 4
          \end{array}\right]
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Rewrite as:
        <md>
          <mrow>x \amp \amp \amp \amp \amp \amp = \amp \amp -5</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp \amp y \amp \amp \amp \amp = \amp \amp 2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp \amp \amp \amp z \amp \amp = \amp \amp 4</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>Solution: <m>x = -5</m>, <m>y = 2</m>, <m>z = 4</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <example xml:id="ex-u2-back-substitution">
    <title>Solving a system using back substitution (U2-LO1, U2-LO2)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        We solve the system represented by the following augmented matrix using back substitution.
      </p>
      <p>
        <me>
          \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
          1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 4\\
          0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2\\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -3
          \end{array}\right]
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>We write the corresponding equations:</p>
      <p>
        <me>
          \begin{array}{ccccccc}
          x \amp + \amp 2y \amp + \amp z  \amp = \amp 4\\
          \amp   \amp y  \amp + \amp 2z \amp = \amp 2\\
          \amp   \amp    \amp   \amp z  \amp = \amp -3
          \end{array}
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Substitute <m>z = -3</m> into the second equation:
      </p>
      <p>
        <me>
          \begin{array}{ccccccc}
          \amp \amp y  \amp + \amp 2(-3) \amp = \amp 2\\
          \amp \amp y  \amp \amp \amp = \amp 8
          \end{array}
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Substitute <m>y = 8</m> and <m>z = -3</m> into the first equation:
      </p>
      <p>
        <me>
          \begin{array}{ccccccc}
          x \amp + \amp 2(8) \amp + \amp (-3) \amp = \amp 4\\
          x \amp \amp \amp \amp \amp = \amp -9
          \end{array}
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Solution: <m>x = -9</m>, <m>y = 8</m>, and <m>z = -3</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>


  <definition xml:id="def-1-2-row-echelon-form"> 
    <title>Row echelon form and reduced row echelon form</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        An <m>m\times n</m> matrix <m>M</m> is in <term>row echelon form</term> (REF) if it satisfies the following properties:
        <ol>
          <li>All <em>zero rows</em>, if there are any, appear at the <em>bottom</em> of the matrix.</li>
          <li>The <em>leftmost nonzero entry</em> in any nonzero row is a 1. This entry is called the <term>leading one</term> of its row.</li>
          <li>For each nonzero row, the leading one appears to the <em>right and below</em> any leading ones in <em>preceding</em> rows.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>
      <p>
        We say <m>M</m> is in <term>reduced row echelon form</term> (RREF) if <m>M</m> is in REF and also satisfies:
        <ul>
          <li>all columns containing leading ones contain no other non-zero entries.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    A matrix in row echelon form appears as a staircase (or <em>echelon</em>) pattern of leading ones descending from the upper left corner of the matrix. <xref ref="fig-staircase" /> gives a schematic of a matrix in row echelon form, where the asterisks denote arbitrary constants.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-staircase">
    <caption>A schematic of a matrix in row echelon form.</caption>
    <image width="60%" xml:id="matrix-in-row-echelon-form">
      <shortdescription>
        A staircase schematic of a matrix in row echelon form.
      </shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          The matrix is drawn with leading <m>1</m>s stepping down and to the right from row to row. A thick line connects the pivot positions, forming an irregular staircase shape, and asterisks to the right of the pivots represent arbitrary entries.
        </p>
      </description>
      <latex-image>
       \begin{tikzpicture}
       % Matrix entries
       \matrix[matrix of math nodes, left delimiter={[}, right delimiter={]}, row sep=0.5em, column sep=1.2em] (m) {
       0 \amp 1 \amp * \amp * \amp * \amp * \amp * \\
       0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp * \amp * \amp * \\
       0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp * \amp * \\
       0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
       0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
       };

       % Staircase outline
       \draw[thick] ($($(m-1-2.north west)$)!0.5!($(m-1-1.north east)$)$)
       --($($($(m-2-2.north west)$)!0.5!($(m-2-1.north east)$)$)!0.5!($($(m-1-2.south west)$)!0.5!($(m-1-1.south east)$)$)$)
       --($($($(m-2-4.north west)$)!0.5!($(m-2-3.north east)$)$)!0.5!($($(m-1-4.south west)$)!0.5!($(m-1-3.south east)$)$)$)
       --($($($(m-3-4.north west)$)!0.5!($(m-3-3.north east)$)$)!0.5!($($(m-2-4.south west)$)!0.5!($(m-2-3.south east)$)$)$)
       --($($($(m-3-5.north west)$)!0.5!($(m-3-4.north east)$)$)!0.5!($($(m-2-5.south west)$)!0.5!($(m-2-4.south east)$)$)$)
       --($($($(m-4-5.north west)$)!0.5!($(m-4-4.north east)$)$)!0.5!($($(m-3-5.south west)$)!0.5!($(m-3-4.south east)$)$)$)
       --($($($(m-4-7.north west)$)!0.5!($(m-4-6.north east)$)$)!0.5!($($(m-3-7.south west)$)!0.5!($(m-3-6.south east)$)$)$)
       --($($($(m-5-7.north west)$)!0.5!($(m-5-6.north east)$)$)!0.5!($($(m-4-7.south west)$)!0.5!($(m-4-6.south east)$)$)$)
       -- ($($(m-5-7.north east)$)!0.5!($(m-4-7.south east)$)$);
       %($(m-1-2.north west)!0.5!(m-1.1.north east)$) -- ($(m-2-2.north west)!0.5!(m-2-1.north west)$);
       \end{tikzpicture}
     </latex-image>
   </image>
 </figure>

 <activity>
  <title>Identifying REF and RREF matrices (U2-LO2)</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      For each of the following augmented matrices, determine whether it is in row echelon form, reduced row echelon form, both, or neither.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <me>
          A = \left[\begin{array}{cccc|c}
          1 \amp 3 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 2 \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
          \end{array}\right]
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>Both REF and RREF (staircase pattern, 0s in columns with leading ones).</p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <me>
          B = \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
          1 \amp 4 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -2
          \end{array}\right]
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>Not REF (zero row is not a bottom row).</p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <me>
          C = \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
          1 \amp 3 \amp 5 \amp 7 \\
          0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 5 \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 10 \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
          \end{array}\right]
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>REF but not RREF (has nonzero entries above leading ones).</p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <me>
          D = \left[\begin{array}{cccc|c}
          1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
          0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 4 \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 6
          \end{array}\right]
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>Both REF and RREF (has zeros above and below leading ones).</p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <me>
          E = \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
          1 \amp 0 \amp 4 \amp 3 \\
          0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \\
          0 \amp 1 \amp -5 \amp 2 \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
          \end{array}\right]
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>Not REF (third row has a leading one in same column as second row).</p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

<definition>
  <title>Pivot entries, positions, and columns</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      For a matrix <m>M</m> in REF, each leading one is a <term>pivot entry</term>. Its location is a <term>pivot position</term>. A column containing a pivot position is a <term>pivot column</term>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<theorem xml:id="thm-unique-rref">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Every matrix <m>M</m> is <em>row equivalent</em> to a <em>unique</em> matrix in RREF.
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<p xml:id="sec-gaussian-elimination-12">
  The Gaussian Algorithm gives a procedure to find a row-echelon matrix which is row equivalent to any given matrix. The heuristic is to move from top to bottom and outside in until you obtain a matrix in row-echelon form.
</p>

<algorithm xml:id="alg-gaussian-elimination">
  <title>The Gaussian Elimination Algorithm</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Given a matrix, this algorithm will compute a matrix in row echelon form which is row-equivalent to the original matrix.
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>If the matrix consists entirely of zeros, stop — it is already in row-echelon form.</li>
        <li>Otherwise, find the left-most column containing a nonzero entry <m>a</m>, and move the row containing that entry to the top position.</li>
        <li>Now multiply the new top row by <m>1/a</m> so that the row has a leading one.</li>
        <li>By subtracting multiples of that row from rows below it, make each entry below this leading one equal to zero.</li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
      This completes the first row. Now Repeat Steps 1-4 on the matrix consisting of all other rows. The process stops when either no rows remain, or the remaining rows consist entirely of zeros.
    </p>
  </statement>
</algorithm>


<note xml:id="note-u2-row-operation-shorthand">
  <title>Row-operation shorthand</title>
  <p>
    We often record row operations using the symbol <m>R_i</m> for row <m>i</m>. The notation <m>R_i \to R_i + cR_j</m> means to replace row <m>i</m> by row <m>i</m> plus <m>c</m> times row <m>j</m>. All rows not named on the left side stay the same.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><p><m>R_3 \to R_3 - 2R_1</m> means replace row <m>3</m> by row <m>3</m> minus twice row <m>1</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p><m>R_2 \to \frac{1}{5}R_2</m> means multiply row <m>2</m> by <m>1/5</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p><m>R_1 \leftrightarrow R_2</m> means interchange rows <m>1</m> and <m>2</m>.</p></li>
  </ul>
</note>

<activity xml:id="sec-gaussian-elimination-14">
  <title>Finding row echelon forms (U2-LO2)</title>
  <statement>
    <p>Find a row echelon form of the augmented matrix <m>M</m>.</p>
    <me>
      M = \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
      0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
      3 \amp 3 \amp 6 \amp -9 \\
      2 \amp 3 \amp 0 \amp -2
      \end{array}\right]
    </me>
    <p>
      <url href="https://math345-games.github.io/TheEliminationScratchpad/the_elimination_scratchpad.html?v=1&amp;activity=2.2.42&amp;m=0%2C0%2C1%2C2~3%2C3%2C6%2C-9~2%2C3%2C0%2C-2&amp;vars=x%2Cy%2Cz&amp;goal=ref&amp;back=https%3A%2F%2Fmath345-textbook.github.io%2Fsec-u2-systems-geometry-row-reduction.html%23sec-gaussian-elimination-14" visual="The Elimination Scratchpad">Practice this activity in The Elimination Scratchpad</url>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>First nonzero column is column 1. Interchange rows 1 and 2: <m>R_1 \leftrightarrow R_2</m>.</p>
    <me>
      \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
      3 \amp 3 \amp 6 \amp -9 \\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
      2 \amp 3 \amp 0 \amp -2
      \end{array}\right]
    </me>

    <p>Multiply first row by <m>1/3</m>: <m>R_1 \to \frac{1}{3}R_1</m>.</p>
    <me>
      \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
      1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -3 \\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
      2 \amp 3 \amp 0 \amp -2
      \end{array}\right]
    </me>

    <p>Subtract 2 times first row from Row 3: <m>R_3 \to R_3 - 2R_1</m>.</p>
    <me>
      \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
      1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -3 \\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
      0 \amp 1 \amp -4 \amp 4
      \end{array}\right]
    </me>

    <p>Interchange rows 2 and 3: <m>R_2 \leftrightarrow R_3</m>.</p>
    <me>
      \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
      1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -3 \\
      0 \amp 1 \amp -4 \amp 4 \\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2
      \end{array}\right]
    </me>

    <p>This augmented matrix is row equivalent to <m>M</m> and is in REF with leading ones in variable columns 1, 2, and 3.</p>
  </solution>
</activity>

</subsection>
